WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.052
The best file workflows often avoid user-controlled paths altogether.

2
00:00:04.052 --> 00:00:11.227
Instead of accepting a path, the application accepts an opaque file ID,
report ID, attachment ID, or storage record identifier.

3
00:00:11.227 --> 00:00:20.660
The application then looks up a trusted record that contains the approved
storage location, owner, tenant, lifecycle state, content type, and
authorization context.

4
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.516
The user chooses the resource, not the server path.

5
00:00:23.516 --> 00:00:26.041
Allowlisted identifiers reduce ambiguity.

6
00:00:26.041 --> 00:00:30.624
A safe identifier has a known format and maps to a record the application
controls.

7
00:00:30.624 --> 00:00:36.802
Generated storage names reduce the chance that a display name can
influence directories or backend behavior.

8
00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:42.980
Users may still choose friendly display names, but those names should be
metadata, not storage instructions.

9
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:49.956
Separating display names from storage names also makes it easier to rename
files without moving them or weakening controls.

10
00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:53.277
Storage abstraction helps when the backing store changes.

11
00:00:53.277 --> 00:01:00.053
A file may live on a local disk, mounted volume, object storage service,
document store, or generated report repository.

12
00:01:00.053 --> 00:01:06.895
The application should expose a consistent storage policy rather than leak
filesystem layout into routes and templates.

13
00:01:06.895 --> 00:01:15.664
A well-designed storage layer can enforce allowed locations, safe naming,
metadata validation, and consistent access checks across frameworks and
jobs.

14
00:01:15.664 --> 00:01:21.709
Allowlists should be used where names, extensions, content categories, or
storage areas are constrained.

15
00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:28.485
A broad blocklist of dangerous-looking names is brittle because file
systems and storage services have many edge cases.

16
00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:35.926
A narrow allowlist for expected identifiers, generated names, approved
extensions, and known storage records is easier to review.

17
00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:41.240
The practical goal is to make unsafe paths impossible to express through
the public interface.
